From b47883bf22777be6de4a8f737c28aabcc25662ca Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andy1210 <andy1210@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 08:49:12 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 8/8] arm64: dts: qcom: sm8350: Put the GPU clock controller
 under CX

Nothing on the Linux side votes a CX level for the GPU subsystem: the
GPU clock controller has no power domain, so GPU_CX_GDSC (the GMU, its
200 MHz clock, the GPU SMMU) and GPU_GX_GDSC have no parent, and the
GMU's own CX vote only exists once its firmware runs. The GMU OPP
table asks for MIN_SVS at 200 MHz, but that level is only sent to the
GMU as data; CX itself is whatever the other APPS consumers vote,
which after the rpmhpd sync_state can be nothing while the phone is
idle. The downstream Lahaina DT supplies the GPU clock controller
from CX and MX and parents the CX GDSC on the CX level (and the GX
GDSC on GFX).

With the clock controller in the CX domain both GDSCs become CX
subdomains, so a powered GPU_CX_GDSC votes CX at its first active
level (MIN_SVS), the GMU OPP's level. MX follows CX in rpmhpd.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8350.dtsi | 1 +
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8350.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8350.dtsi
index ad7b5e462..dd9bc8ada 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8350.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8350.dtsi
@@ -2403,6 +2403,7 @@ gpucc: clock-controller@3d90000 {
 			clock-names = "bi_tcxo",
 				      "gcc_gpu_gpll0_clk_src",
 				      "gcc_gpu_gpll0_div_clk_src";
+			power-domains = <&rpmhpd RPMHPD_CX>;
 			#clock-cells = <1>;
 			#reset-cells = <1>;
 			#power-domain-cells = <1>;
